  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sauce with a Cause: Creating stable and liveable incomes for smallholder farmers in Kwakyai, Kenya</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Kenya, 40% of tomatoes and even 67% of mangos get wasted due to post-harvest losses. The Ketchup project (TKP, Sauce with a cause) aims to reduce past-harvest losses for small holder farmers in developing countries and help them generate stable and liveable incomes. TKP does this by locally buying and drying tomatoes that would otherwise end up as waste and import these to the Netherlands for further processing into ketchup for the European market. Drying is a cost-effective and robust method to extend shelf-life and equalise harvest losses and adds value for famers. To prepare for future investments and enable scale-up of production, Technical Assistance (DGGF-TA) provides support to set-up a dry hub &#x2013; processing facility in Kenya where farmers can cut, dry, package and label their products according to international standards that allows KTP to buy these at a higher and fair guaranteed price at living wage standards. The project includes support to set-up this dry hub, to provide training to reduce food losses, help produce year-round and improve processing standards and to prepare for certification (Global SAP and FSCC), as well as developing production and sales for the Dutch market through a guarantee for the first shipments of dried tomatoes and mangos. The project is ongoing and currently supports 30 direct jobs, 129 indirect jobs and provides knowledge transfer by having trained 134 people. The first bottles of Ketchup using Kenyan dried tomatoes and mangos have successfully been produced in the Netherlands and more impact is expected. Upon successful completion, the model will be replicated and investments in additional dry hubs in Kenya will be prepared.</narrative>
  </description>
